          "login": "KageBinary",
          "id": 107007243,
          "type": "User",
          "html_url": "https://github.com/KageBinary"
        },
        "state": "APPROVED",
        "body": "Thanks for contributing! This one is my favourite of the four — small, and it fixes a real correctness bug rather than papering over it.\n\nThe premise is right: `--kind` is a **filter**, not a **tiebreaker**. The old shortcut treated \"the user named a kind\" as consent to pick arbitrarily among everything matching that kind, so two same-named classes resolved to whichever sorted first — and because it returned `\"exact\"` as the resolution mode, the output actively asserted confidence it did not have. Silently analysing the wrong symbol is worse than asking.\n\nI checked the things a change like this usually breaks, and they hold:\n\n- **`--pick` still reaches the user.** `buildAmbiguous` already emits `\"Use --pick <n> or --path to disambiguate.\"`, so the new ambiguous path is actionable rather than a dead end.\n- **The earlier exits still cover the single-match case.** `--kind` narrowing to one candidate returns via the `unique.length === 1` branch with `resolutionMode(best, opts)`, so the ordinary \"kind disambiguates\" flow is unchanged.\n- **No accidental auto-pick downstream.** With the shortcut gone, `--kind` queries now fall through to the preferred-kind and container-kind rules. For two same-kind candidates the kind ranks are equal, so the `topTierKindRanks.every(...)` guard does not fire, and `CONTAINER_KINDS.has(bestKind) && !CONTAINER_KINDS.has(secondKind)` is false when both kinds are identical. Ambiguity is preserved, which is what your test asserts.\n\nBoth new tests are testing the actual behaviour change (ambiguity preserved; `--pick 2` selects by index and carries the right path), not just the code path.\n\nApproving. No changes requested.\n",
        "html_url": "https://github.com/ix-infrastructure/Ix/pull/380#pullrequestreview-4899750103",
        "submitted_at": "2026-08-10T18:19:55Z",
        "commit_id": "b89046144f0a8cd960a7bbee22b3a12145c6c82e",
